.EE
.SH SEMANTICS
.TP
\f[B]ranges\f[R]
CIDRs, IPv4 and IPv6.
With no entries, any address passes.
With entries, a target outside all of them is dropped before any
connection.
A malformed CIDR is skipped without error, so what only it covered is
dropped.
.TP
\f[B]ports.allow / ports.deny\f[R]
Deny wins over allow.
If \f[B]allow\f[R] is empty, all ports are allowed subject to
\f[B]deny\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]dial.blocked_numbers\f[R]
Numbers refused by \f[B]dial\f[R], exact or as a prefix, on top of the
hard\-coded block of numbers of 3 digits or fewer.
The dialled number and each entry are normalised the same way (drop
\(dq+\(dq, a leading \(dq00\(dq and punctuation), so \(dq+34112\(dq,
\(dq0034112\(dq and \(dq34112\(dq block the same range (PITF\-098).
.TP
\f[B]protocols.allow / protocols.deny\f[R]
Which plugins may run.
An entry matches a plugin\(aqs name or its protocol, the part of the
name before the first \(dq\-\(dq: \f[B]s7\f[R] covers s7 and
s7\-exposure, \f[B]s7\-exposure\f[R] only that plugin, and
\f[B]opcuahttps\f[R] is a name of its own.
Case is ignored.
Deny wins over allow; an empty \f[B]allow\f[R] lets through every plugin
not denied, and a non\-empty one must list \f[B]banner\f[R] for banner
to run.
A plugin named with \f[B]\-\-plugin\f[R] (or in a dashboard job) that
the lists refuse stops the scan (exit 77, or a failed job); without
names the refused plugins are left out, and \f[B]scan\f[R] says which on
stderr.
\f[B]discover\f[R] does not sweep the ports of refused plugins.
The verbs count as a plugin: \f[B]opcua probe\-anon\f[R] and
\f[B]probe\-write\f[R] as opcua\-exposure, the three \f[B]s7\f[R] probes
as s7\-exposure, \f[B]harvest\f[R] as its sub\-verb (telnet, ftp,
http\-basic, snmp), \f[B]creds\-check http\f[R] as http\-basic,
\f[B]write modbus send\f[R] as modbus and \f[B]proxy listen\f[R] as its
\f[B]\-\-plugin\f[R].
\f[B]exploit run\f[R] and \f[B]plaintext\-check\f[R] name no plugin, so
only ranges and ports apply to them.
Families go by name only: \f[B]deny: [s7]\f[R] does not cover
\f[B]mms\f[R], which also speaks COTP on port 102; list it too.
An entry that matches no plugin (a typo such as \f[B]s7\-\f[R]) changes
nothing and no command warns about it.
.TP
\f[B]binds.allow\f[R]
When set and \f[B]serve\f[R] is given \f[B]\-\-scope\f[R], the bind
address must be one of these (exit 77 otherwise); an empty list allows
any bind (PITF\-098).
A non\-loopback bind is still governed by its own flags
(\f[B]\-\-tls\-cert\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tls\-key\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-i\-know\-what\-im\-doing\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]canary\f[R]
Parsed, not applied.
.PP
\f[B]ranges\f[R] and \f[B]ports\f[R] apply to the address and port each
command connects to.
A command with a list (\f[B]scan\f[R], \f[B]serve\f[R],
\f[B]discover\f[R], \f[B]tui \-\-input\f[R]) drops what falls outside; a
command with one \f[B]\-\-target\f[R] refuses it with exit 77 before
connecting.
A read\-only command given a name resolves it once, checks that address
and connects to it, so the name cannot resolve elsewhere after the
check; \f[B]creds\-check http\f[R] does the same and keeps the name in
the Host header.
\f[B]write modbus send\f[R], \f[B]exploit run\f[R] and \f[B]proxy
listen\f[R] need \f[B]\-\-target\f[R] as an IP:port with
\f[B]\-\-scope\f[R] (exit 64 otherwise): their confirm token covers the
target as typed.
.PP
A dropped target is not probed and leaves no trace: there is no
per\-target message and no audit event.
When no target is left, \f[B]scan\f[R], \f[B]discover\f[R] and \f[B]tui
\-\-input\f[R] exit with 66.
